##: {{ux|en|a '''knotty''' problem}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1 Q1|act=II|scene=v|page=38|passage=VVhy thou clay braind guts, thou '''knotty'''-pated foole, thou horeſon obſcene greaſie tallovv-catch.|footer=''Knotty-pated'' apparently means “stupid”,<ref name="OED"/> but the ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' also points out that it may be associated with ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=Rbw2AQAAMAAJ&pg=PP34 not-pated]'' (“with hair cut short”)<ref>{{cite-book|author=N[orman] F[rancis] Blake|authorlink=Norman Blake (academic)|entry=HEAD, words for|title=[[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]]’s Non-standard English: A Dictionary of His Informal Language|series=Student Shakespeare Library|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Continuum International Publishing Group|Continuum]]|year=2006|page=166|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LsRhMOlfKsMC&pg=PA166|isbn=978-0-8264-9123-7|passage=with close-cropped hair}}; {{cite-web|author=David Crystal|authorlink=David Crystal|author2=Ben Crystal|authorlink2=Ben Crystal|entry=not-pated, ''adj.''|entryurl=https://www.shakespeareswords.com/Public/Glossary.aspx?id=11366|work=Shakespeare’s Words|year=2017–|passage=crop-headed, short-haired}}.</ref> which appears earlier in the same scene.|brackets=on}}
##* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Regiment of Health. XXX.|page=188|passage=As for the Paſſions and Studies of the Minde, Auoid Enuie; Anxious Feares; Anger fretting invvards; Subtill and '''knottie''' Inquiſitions; Ioyes, and Exhilarations in Exceſſe; Sadneſſe not Communicated.}}
##* {{RQ:Swift Tale of a Tub|section=IX|sectionname=A Digression Concerning the Original, the Use and Improvement of Madness in a Commonwealth|pages=169–170|pageref=170|passage=The preſent Argument is the moſt abſtracted that ever I engaged in, it ſtrains my Faculties to their higheſt Stretch; and I deſire the Reader to attend with utmoſt perpenſity; For, I now proceed to unravel this '''knotty''' Point.}}
##* {{RQ:Pope Works|volume=I|title=January and May|page=208|passage=He ſaid; the reſt in diff'rent parts divide, / The '''knotty''' point vvas urg'd on either ſide; / Marriage, the theme on vvhich they all declaim'd, / Some prais'd vvith vvit, and ſome vvith reaſon blam'd.}}
## Of an [[austere]] or [[hard#Adjective|hard]] [[nature#Noun|nature]]; [[rugged#Adjective|rugged]].
##* {{RQ:Ascham Scholemaster|folio=5|verso=1|folioend=6|folioref=5|versoref=1|passage=[A] witte in youth, that is not ouer dulle, heauie, '''knottie''' and lumpiſhe, but hard, rough, and though ſomwhat ſtaffiſhe, {{...}} ſuch a witte I ſay, if it be, at the firſt well handled by the mother, and rightlie ſmothed and wrought as it ſhould, not ouerwhartlie, and against the wood, by the ſcholemaſter, both for learning, and hole courſe of liuing, proueth alwaies the beſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Divorce|chapter=The Preface|page=6|passage={{...}} I may erre perhaps in ſoothing my ſelfe that this preſent truth reviv'd, vvill deſerve on all hands to be not ſiniſterly receiv'd, {{...}} but vvith a ſmooth and pleaſing leſſon, vvhich receiv'd hath the vertue to ſoften and diſpell rooted and '''knotty''' ſorrovves: {{...}}}}

